1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
 As we will see in this course, there are different types of cloud computing,

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:06,240
 and it is important for us to be able to recognize them.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:11,280
 The first one is called infrastructure as a service or IAAS.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,480
 This is to provide the building blocks for cloud IT.

5
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:21,760
 With this IAAS, we're going to provide networking, computers, and data storage space in its raw form.

6
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,480
 And using these building blocks, deals, building legos,

7
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,920
 we're going to be given a very high level of flexibility,

8
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:34,080
 and we can easily understand how we can migrate from traditional on-premises IT to the cloud.

9
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:37,440
 Then we're going to get platform as a service.

10
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:42,960
 In this, we're going to remove the need for your organization to manage the underlying infrastructure.

11
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:47,200
 And you can just focus on the deployment and management of your applications.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:52,080
 And then the one step even further is software as a service or SaaS.

13
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:57,280
 This is a completed product that is going to be run and managed by the service provider.

14
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,840
 So if you want to compare all these things,

15
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,520
 well, it's like an example.

16
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,160
 On-premises, you're going to manage everything.

17
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:07,920
 So your applications, your data, your runtime, your middleware,

18
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:12,960
 the operating system, virtualization, servers, storage, and networking.

19
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:13,840
 And that's a lot.

20
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,440
 With IAAS, so infrastructure as a service,

21
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:22,160
 we're going to manage the application, the data, the runtime, the middleware, and the OS,

22
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:26,320
 but all the virtualization, servers, storage, and networking

23
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:29,840
 are going to be managed by others in our case AWS.

24
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:33,360
 With platform as a service, we manage even less.

25
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:38,080
 So everything from the runtime to the networking is managed by AWS.

26
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,680
 And the only thing we care about when we use a platform as a service

27
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,560
 is our application and our data.

28
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:47,440
 And finally, well, if you're using software as a service,

29
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,800
 everything is going to be managed by AWS.

30
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,400
 So how does it translate?

31
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:56,080
 Well, with IAAS, we can use Amazon EC2 on AWS,

32
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:00,240
 but we have other services such as Google Cloud, Azure, Rackspace, Digital Ocean,

33
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:04,960
 and Linux, which will provide us a cloud computing infrastructure as a service.

34
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:09,120
 Platform as a service also exists on AWS with Elastic Beanstalk,

35
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:11,760
 and we'll see all these services, obviously, in this course.

36
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:17,120
 And outside of AWS, we have Heroku, Google App Engine, and Windows Azure.

37
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:23,200
 For software as a service, we also have this on AWS that represents many services of AWS,

38
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,960
 for example, recognition when we want to do some machine learning.

39
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:30,320
 But we've been using it as well in the real world with Google Apps,

40
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:34,160
 such as Gmail, or Dropbox, or Zoom for your meetings.

41
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:36,080
 So the clouds have different flavors,

42
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:39,840
 but one thing is common is that the pricing is very different from what you know.

43
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:43,520
 AWS has three pricing fundamentals,

44
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,080
 and it will follow the pay as you go pricing model.

45
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:49,040
 So for the compute, and that represents various services,

46
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,000
 we're going to pay for the exact compute time.

47
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,960
 For the storage, we're going to pay for the exact amount of data stored in the cloud.

48
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:01,520
 And for the networking, we're going to only pay when the data leaves the cloud.

49
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,400
 Any data that goes into the cloud is free.

50
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:09,120
 And this solves the expensive issue of traditional IT,

51
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:11,440
 because now we only pay exactly what we need,

52
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,600
 and so we have huge cost savings ahead of us.

53
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:18,800
 So that's it for this lecture, and the next lecture will be having a deeper dive on AWS.

